":"vessel","note":"1 shared story or event","stub":false},{"slug":"duluth-ore-dock-company","title":"Duluth Ore Dock Company","type":"organization","note":"worked at","stub":false},{"slug":"anders-s-sea-chest","title":"Anders's sea chest","type":"object","note":"owned by","stub":true}],"open_questions":[{"text":"The sources disagree: Age at the crossing: the family says sixteen [S1 00:00:05], the manifest says 17 (R001). Both kept.","from":"E001"},{"text":"Anders works on the ore boats at Duluth (family account): low confidence (Family account; follows E002)","from":"E003"},{"text":"Anders Calder dies in Duluth: low confidence (Family account)","from":"E010"}],"beyond":{"items":[],"suggestions":[]},"coords":null,"routes":["facts/records/calder/ottavia_1900_track.csv"],"genealogy":"anders-calder","history":[{"at":"2026-09-20T10:00:00-05:00","by":"me","fields":["lead"]}],"wiki":"<div class=\"encyc\"><table class=\"winfobox\"><caption>Anders Calder</caption><tr><th>Born</th><td>about 1883, near Aalesund, Norway</td></tr><tr><th>Died</th><td>1951, Duluth, Minnesota (family account)</td></tr><tr><th>Emigrated</th><td>1900, Liverpool to Quebec, S.S. Ottavia</td></tr><tr><th>Occupation</th><td>Ore-boat hand; dock foreman</td></tr><tr><th>Spouse</th><td><a href=\"#familypedia/signe-calder\">Signe Calder</a></td></tr><tr><th>Children</th><td><a href=\"#familypedia/henrik-calder\">Henrik Calder</a></td></tr><tr><th>Relatives</th><td><a href=\"#familypedia/ruth-calder\">Ruth Calder</a> (granddaughter)</td></tr></table><p><b>Anders Calder</b> (about 1883 – 1951) was a Norwegian emigrant who worked the iron-ore boats and docks at Duluth, Minnesota, and the grandfather of <a href=\"#familypedia/ruth-calder\">Ruth Calder</a>.<sup class=\"wref\"><a href=\"#ref-1\" id=\"cite-1-1\">[1]</a></sup><sup class=\"wref\"><a href=\"#ref-2\" id=\"cite-2-2\">[2]</a></sup></p><nav class=\"wtoc\"><b>Contents</b><ol><li><a href=\"#emigration\">Emigration</a></li><li><a href=\"#duluth\">Duluth</a></li><li><a href=\"#possessions\">Possessions</a></li><li><a href=\"#in-the-stories\">In the stories</a></li></ol></nav><h2 id=\"emigration\">Emigration</h2><p>The family remembered that Anders crossed \"on a ship when he was sixteen\", wearing one pair of boots the whole way.<sup class=\"wref\"><a href=\"#ref-2\" id=\"cite-2-2\">[2]</a></sup> The passenger manifest of the S.S. Ottavia, which arrived at Quebec from Liverpool on 21 May 1900, lists \"Calder, Anders\", aged 17, of Aalesund, bound for Duluth.<sup class=\"wref\"><a href=\"#ref-1\" id=\"cite-1-2\">[1]</a></sup> The record and the family account differ by a year; both are kept.</p><h2 id=\"duluth\">Duluth</h2><p>He worked on the ore boats and then at the docks.<sup class=\"wref\"><a href=\"#ref-3\" id=\"cite-3-3\">[3]</a></sup> The 1910 census shows him in Duluth with his wife, <a href=\"#familypedia/signe-calder\">Signe Calder</a>, and their son <a href=\"#familypedia/henrik-calder\">Henrik Calder</a>, born that year.<sup class=\"wref\"><a href=\"#ref-4\" id=\"cite-4-4\">[4]</a></sup> In October 1938 the payroll of the <a href=\"#familypedia/duluth-ore-dock-company\">Duluth Ore Dock Company</a> listed \"A. Calder\" as dock foreman at Dock No. 6, with his son as chute tender.<sup class=\"wref\"><a href=\"#ref-5\" id=\"cite-5-5\">[5]</a></sup> He died in 1951, according to the family.<sup class=\"wref\"><a href=\"#ref-6\" id=\"cite-6-6\">[6]</a></sup></p><h2 id=\"possessions\">Possessions</h2><p>His painted pine sea chest, with his initials on the lid, survives with a great-grandson.<sup class=\"wref\"><a href=\"#ref-7\" id=\"cite-7-7\">[7]</a></sup></p><h2 id=\"in-the-stories\">In the stories</h2><p><a href=\"#stories?read=1\">Others in the Family</a>.</p><h2 id=\"references\">References</h2><ol class=\"wrefs\"><li id=\"ref-1\">Passenger manifest, S.S. Ottavia, Liverpool to Quebec, May 1900 (record R001). <a href=\"https://example.org/demo/ottavia-1900\" rel=\"noopener\">https://example.org/demo/ottavia-1900</a></li><li id=\"ref-2\">Ruth Calder, recorded interview, session S1, 00:00:05 (demo recording).</li><li id=\"ref-3\">Ruth Calder, recorded interview, session S1, 00:00:21 (demo recording).</li><li id=\"ref-4\">Federal census 1910, Duluth, ward 4, sheet 12 (record R002). <a href=\"https://example.org/demo/census-1910-duluth\" rel=\"noopener\">https://example.org/demo/census-1910-duluth</a></li><li id=\"ref-5\">\"Payroll ledger, Duluth Ore Dock Company, Dock No. 6\", October 1938 (record R003). <a href=\"https://example.org/demo/ore-dock-payroll-1938\" rel=\"noopener\">https://example.org/demo/ore-dock-payroll-1938</a></li><li id=\"ref-6\">Family timeline, event E010 (family account).</li><li id=\"ref-7\">Family records catalogue, item R006 (Anders's sea chest).</li></ol></div>","wiki_title":"Anders Calder"}